  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Nagiyev gathers the kitchen staff to announce the mass firings. Senya and Fedya discuss the depressing situation. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ребята, нам придётся расстаться с вами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Куда вы собрались так рано?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нас уволили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Как уволили? Тебя тоже?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, мы все уходим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "А что сказал шеф?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф ничего не сделал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы часто шагаем в пропасть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Это из-за самоуверенности?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, мы слишком много хотели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот ваш расчёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Только двадцать тысяч?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Остальное будет в конце месяца.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ребята, нам придётся расстаться с вами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Guys, we will have to part ways with you.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'to break up', which directs the action back onto the subject. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Куда вы собрались так рано?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where are you off to so early?' The question word for 'where to' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'early'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нас уволили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We got fired.' Fedya links 'us' directly with 'to fire' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Как уволили? Тебя тоже?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How fired? You too?' Senya uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'too', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, мы все уходим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, we are all leaving.' Fedya combines 'yes' with 'are leaving' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "А что сказал шеф?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what did the chef say?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф ничего не сделал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The chef did nothing.' Fedya combines 'chef' with 'did' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы часто шагаем в пропасть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We often walk into the abyss.' Notice how 'we' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'to disappear'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Это из-за самоуверенности?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is this because of overconfidence?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'overconfidence' is enough to make it a question. And Fedya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, мы слишком много хотели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, we wanted too much.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'yes' with 'wanted'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот ваш расчёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is your payout.' Dmitry uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'final pay', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Только двадцать тысяч?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Only twenty thousand?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'only' directly with 'thousand'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Остальное будет в конце месяца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The rest will be at the end of the month.' Dmitry combines 'remaining' with 'months' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
